WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.040
At a panel I was hosting during&nbsp;
MagicCon Vegas a few weeks ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.800
an audience member asked a question&nbsp;
that really got me thinking:

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.080
“I’ve been trying to build a hidden&nbsp;
commander and I don’t know how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:18.160
make it work. It’s the Sibsig Ceremony: three&nbsp;
black, creature spells cost 2 less to cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.520
and whenever a creature you&nbsp;
control enters, if you cast it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:30.240
destroy it and then you get a token…. How do&nbsp;
I make weird niche hidden commanders work?”

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.280
Hidden commanders are an interesting subject, but&nbsp;
they definitely aren’t all made equal. I responded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.320
to this person’s question, and my cohost’s answer,&nbsp;
by saying that some classically-touted examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.040
of build-around cards, such as Sunforger,&nbsp;
are deceptively simple to pull off because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:49.840
the cards that they work with don’t eat up that&nbsp;
much deck space. Red and white instants that cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.880
four or less mana are good, and you’ll already&nbsp;
want some of them in your deck. Equipments are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:58.720
also good, and you should be able to find other&nbsp;
options to grab if you want to run tutors for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.000
your Forger. However, for a secret commander that&nbsp;
typically requires more deck space to support it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.520
as The Sibsig Ceremony often does, you’ll need to&nbsp;
thread the needle more carefully in deckbuilding.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.400
“The second question would be are there analogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:19.120
are there things that do what you want to do&nbsp;
with the Sibsig Ceremony—kind of—that aren’t it….&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.280
if you're dedicating a lot of deck space to&nbsp;
this card, what else can work with those cards?”

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:38.400
And, that felt like pretty good advice to&nbsp;
give generally—cards don’t always get drawn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:42.480
and sometimes get removed. I’d tangled&nbsp;
with The Sibsig Ceremony myself a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.480
a year prior for a video building a deck&nbsp;
based around cards chosen by my patrons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.960
so I had some familiarity with that card in&nbsp;
particular and its challenges, but I’m by no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.200
means an expert. That deck was a little on the&nbsp;
goofy side, and the card isn’t really my first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.200
choice as a build-around in the first place, so&nbsp;
I figured it could be somebody else’s job to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.640
a scholar on The Sibsig Ceremony in particular.&nbsp;
However, that plan was upended about a week later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:09.040
when I was spinning a wheel to decide what&nbsp;
deck I’d build for the Magic Mirror Podcast.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.200
[Magic Mirror audio]

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.520
The set of wheels I created spun me into building&nbsp;
a $60 budget deck with 50 or more subtypes in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:33.520
way centered around The Sibsig Ceremony, this old&nbsp;
nemesis of mine. At this point, I just accepted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.000
that I needed to create a full-effort build-around&nbsp;
for this card in order to shake whatever curse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.040
had caused it to follow me. And to be clear,&nbsp;
I do mean an honest to god bracket 3 deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.040
maybe a little janky by choice and by nature&nbsp;
of the requirements going into this deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:52.000
but no jankier than that and with plenty of&nbsp;
powerful plays up its sleeve. In the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.240
of doing precisely this, I ended up landing on&nbsp;
two principles that should guide anybody building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.040
a deck centered around a card in their 99, one&nbsp;
of these being something I directly identified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.200
at the panel and the other being one I kind&nbsp;
of alluded to more indirectly and have since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.520
developed further. These principles are (1) find&nbsp;
analogues for your central card, and (2) minimize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.280
the footprint of cards that synergize with your&nbsp;
central card, with both principles stemming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.840
the essential fact that you simply won’t always&nbsp;
have access to your hidden commander card. So in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.920
this video, I’m going to talk you through my&nbsp;
process of building a Sibsig Ceremony deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.892
and in doing so demonstrate how these two rules&nbsp;
produced a successful—hidden-commander—deck.

00:03:29.892 --> 00:03:29.920
Aligning big-mana incentives

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.480
To start with, let’s discuss the nature of hidden&nbsp;
commanders a bit, and why The Sibsig Ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.920
is one of the trickier ones. The phrase “hidden&nbsp;
commander” simply refers to a deck where one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:45.120
the cards in the 99 is built around more heavily&nbsp;
than the commander itself. My Sharuum Astral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.120
Slide deck is an example of this, where Astral&nbsp;
Slide is a key piece of how the deck works and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.000
Sharuum plays more of a secondary role in adding&nbsp;
flexibility and resilience to the deck. This type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.720
of deck usually involves some number of tutors to&nbsp;
find the core card, although whether or not a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:03.520
tutors for its hidden commander every game might&nbsp;
depend on the nature of the card a bit. Astral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.320
Slide is a ridiculously powerful lategame tool,&nbsp;
so I’ll almost always want to find a copy of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.200
card at some point even if I survived the early&nbsp;
to midgame without it. For purposes of adhering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.200
to principle 1—having analogues—Astral Slide&nbsp;
makes my job easy because it has a functionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.920
identical card as well as a similar enchantment&nbsp;
in another color, effectively giving me three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.560
copies of my key card. There’s no need to get&nbsp;
creative finding cards that do something similar;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.440
they’re just handed to me. Other hidden commanders&nbsp;
make principle #2 the easy one, allowing a player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.120
to minimize the footprint of the cards that&nbsp;
work with their central card quite easily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.440
and not feel like their deck space is being&nbsp;
gobbled up. As I mentioned earlier, Sunforger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.600
is a great build-around because the cards it&nbsp;
works with span a wide range of different roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.680
and since they don’t even need to be in hand for&nbsp;
Sunforger to synergize with them, the challenge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:55.440
figuring out the right number to guarantee setup&nbsp;
and payoff lining up is basically nonexistent. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.600
these are two secret commanders where one of the&nbsp;
two principles I mentioned earlier is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:04.800
guaranteed, easing a bit of the challenge of&nbsp;
deckbuilding. However, some hidden commanders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:10.480
don’t make either of these two principles easy,&nbsp;
and this brings us to The Sibsig Ceremony.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.680
Let’s start, as I did when building this deck, by&nbsp;
tackling what this card does. The first line of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:21.680
text is 100% upside—it makes our creature spells&nbsp;
cost 2 less. That’s a pretty great effect for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.880
three mana. Urza’s Incubator and Semblance Anvil&nbsp;
come the closest to this rate among artifact-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.760
reducers, but each of these has a substantial&nbsp;
downside or limitation. So does The Sibsig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.400
Ceremony, though: the second line of text destroys&nbsp;
creatures when they enter if they were cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.600
and creates a 2/2 zombie. This does place a&nbsp;
serious limitation on what’s going to make sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.320
to do with this card, though it’s definitely&nbsp;
not an insurmountable limitation. When I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.560
searching for a commander for this card, I knew I&nbsp;
wanted it to be something on the more generic side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:55.040
to work out with the other restrictions I’d been&nbsp;
given, and I also knew I wanted it to be something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:00.400
that combines relatively easily with the card&nbsp;
to produce positive outcomes. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.440
I wanted something low-footprint, a commander&nbsp;
that just kinda works with The Sibsig Ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:10.480
without me needing to put in a lot of extra effort&nbsp;
or bring in additional cards. This is how I landed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.520
on Athreos, Shroud-Veiled. The card is 6 mana,&nbsp;
it’s indestructible even when I have it fully&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.880
online via devotion, and it has an ability that&nbsp;
works well alongside ETBs and on-death effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.520
among creatures I play. Even without anything&nbsp;
else on my side of the board, the card is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.600
pretty solid if I get it into play turn 4 off&nbsp;
of a turn 3 Ceremony and start throwing coins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.680
on opposing creatures, effectively making the&nbsp;
card a Worn Powerstone for this precise function.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:40.240
To go along with this, I grabbed a handful of&nbsp;
other indestructible and pseudo-indestructible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.640
creatures: Erebos Bleak-Hearted as another&nbsp;
God that works well with my zombie tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:50.080
Yahenni as a sac outlet, Breathkeeper Seraph as&nbsp;
a unique soulbond creature that can save itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:55.840
and others, and all four in-color Myojins.&nbsp;
These are 8 mana spirits that enter with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:00.160
indestructible counter and can remove this&nbsp;
counter to do a variety of powerful effects.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.320
So in these instances we’re taking advantage of&nbsp;
The Sibsig Ceremony’s powerful ramp potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.160
with creatures that don’t care about the&nbsp;
rest of its text box, and already amongst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.640
these indestructible creatures we can see a&nbsp;
lot of points of cohesion. Erebos + Myojin of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:17.760
Blooming Dawn can draw a billion cards, and any&nbsp;
big Erebos plays are assisted by a sac outlet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:23.120
like Yahenni. Athreos Shroud-Veiled and Myojin&nbsp;
of Grim Betrayal both work fabulously alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:28.000
conventional board wipes, as does Breathkeeper&nbsp;
Seraph to a lesser extent, and Myojin of Cleansing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:33.120
Fire is a boardwipe. The picture that’s being&nbsp;
painted so far is of a big mana, controlling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.040
aristocrats-oriented deck, and to minimize&nbsp;
friction we’re going to want to expand on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.520
that vision and make sure it’s a consistent&nbsp;
feature of the deck, starting with the mana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.560
The Sibsig Ceremony provides us a lot of mana,&nbsp;
so the first order of business in making sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.000
the deck works without it is adding a lot of&nbsp;
other ramp—finding analogs. Heartless Summoning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:56.720
is the easiest choice here as a similar 2 mana&nbsp;
creature discount + downside sort of effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:02.000
and another solid choice is ramp on ETB creatures&nbsp;
like Solemn Simulacrum, Scampering Surveyor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:07.040
and Kor Cartographer. These work on their own as&nbsp;
an alternate ladder from 4 to 6 mana to get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:11.680
commander and give an immediate coin destination,&nbsp;
and they also function quite enjoyably with The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:16.400
Sibsig Ceremony in play, costing 2 mana less&nbsp;
and getting their 2/2 bodies replaced with 2/2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.440
zombies. Past these, I chose to add Crowded&nbsp;
Crypt as a synergistic rock, a Sol Ring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.600
Myr Convert for subtype purposes—The Wheel decreed&nbsp;
that this deck needs to have 50 or more subtypes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:31.200
remember—and seven other two mana rocks to round&nbsp;
things out. This deck ended up pretty high-curved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.098
so playing a mana rock is really what we&nbsp;
want to be doing with our 2 mana turn.

00:08:35.098 --> 00:08:35.120
Pairings and analogues

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.680
So okay, step 1 of this was making sure that this&nbsp;
deck can make use of The Sibsig Ceremony’s mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:45.360
generation, and that it has plenty of other ways&nbsp;
to generate mana in the card’s absence. Great. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.240
let’s think a little harder about this second&nbsp;
line of text—it’s intended to be a downside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:54.400
but does it have to be? The biggest area&nbsp;
where this isn’t a downside is when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:09:00.400
to creatures that we actively want to die. Take&nbsp;
Junji the Midnight Sky—both the discard and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.040
reanimate modes of its death trigger are solid&nbsp;
three mana effects, and with the Sibsig Ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.040
you get that plus a zombie and a death trigger and&nbsp;
a thing in your graveyard you can use for other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:14.800
purposes. More on those things later. Same with&nbsp;
Ao—one mode is a sort of Collected Company effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:19.760
the other is a big amplification to our board if&nbsp;
we’re already loaded with zombies. Both great to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:24.160
get for three mana, and even better with some&nbsp;
extra goodies tacked on top. We’ve also got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:28.480
Erebos Bleak-Hearted and Dark Prophecy as&nbsp;
powerful draw engines when creatures die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:34.400
which pair nicely with both zombie tokens and our&nbsp;
nontoken creatures meeting an untimely demise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:39.280
All of this on-death stuff goes great with The&nbsp;
Sibsig Ceremony, though it does also pose a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.680
of a problem: these cards are a lot less good if&nbsp;
we don’t have a way for creatures to die. If we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.560
assume The Sibsig Ceremony will always be in play,&nbsp;
we’re gonna run into some problems when it’s not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:54.240
So, this deck also integrates a number of ways&nbsp;
to sacrifice our creatures as well—analogs for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:59.920
Sibsig Ceremony’s destroy effect. Season of Loss&nbsp;
is a flexible removal tool that can also draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:03.840
Fanatical Devotion is a sac outlet that&nbsp;
can also save creatures from destruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.960
and there are five other cards that do some&nbsp;
sort of sacrificing task. These are perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:13.200
here because they replace the creature-death&nbsp;
portion of what The Sibsig Ceremony does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:18.480
but most of them are also great when paired with&nbsp;
The Sibsig Ceremony, and this is the perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:22.960
relationship to create with a build-around&nbsp;
card. You want the best possible spread of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:28.160
situational usefulness on a card, not just the&nbsp;
one that’s best paired with a hidden commander.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:33.120
The same logic applies to this deck’s 19 ETB&nbsp;
creatures. Each of these will have a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:37.840
a spread when it comes to how effective they&nbsp;
are, but the floor is decently high, making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:44.000
their footprint relatively low. As a baseline,&nbsp;
they’re fine. A deck that’s casting a standalone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:49.280
Simulacrum, a standalone Chupacabra, a standalone&nbsp;
Crabomination—at worst you’re talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.000
bracket 2 midrange deck territory. Bring in The&nbsp;
Sibsig Ceremony and suddenly the power of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.200
jumps a couple notches up from that, particularly&nbsp;
for the 3-4 mana ETB creatures. Those creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:04.240
are around 2/2 already, so The Sibsig Ceremony&nbsp;
basically just looks like a 2 mana discount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:09.040
that shoves their real body into the graveyard.&nbsp;
Speaking of which, this is where the aristocrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:13.920
stuff we mentioned earlier comes into play again.&nbsp;
This deck has five cards that recur creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:18.720
as well as the seven sources of creature sacrifice&nbsp;
I mentioned earlier for when The Sibsig Ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:22.720
isn’t in play to assist creatures with&nbsp;
their journey to the graveyard. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:27.840
when you’re using one of those and have a bit more&nbsp;
flexibility on your timing for creatures dying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:33.040
Athreos also works as a recursion engine, offering&nbsp;
a ton of value generation alongside one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:37.920
deck’s four sources of continual sacrifice. If&nbsp;
you’re getting a Sepulchral Primordial trigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:43.280
every turn, a Crabomination trigger every turn,&nbsp;
or a couple Ashen Rider triggers every turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:47.520
you’re going to be feeling pretty fabulous about&nbsp;
things. The deck also features a few flicker cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:52.800
baked into the deck to enhance the ETB plan&nbsp;
further, as well as an extra spicy inclusion:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:58.160
Mirror of Life Trapping. This is an artifact that&nbsp;
exiles creatures that enter if they were cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:03.440
and then returns them when the next creature&nbsp;
enters after being cast. In one sense this is like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:09.680
a convoluted table-wide ETB doubler, which works&nbsp;
with our ETBs, but it also pairs beautifully with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:14.480
The Sibsig Ceremony. Instead of getting destroyed,&nbsp;
we can order the triggers so that our creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:18.800
get sucked into the mirror instead, and then&nbsp;
they can join their zombie friend whenever the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:23.680
next creature comes into play. It’s actually crazy&nbsp;
to me that this isn’t run in more Sibsig Ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:29.600
decks—Panharmonicon itself is run in 8% of decks&nbsp;
with the card, and yet the number of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:36.000
running Mirror of Life Trapping doesn’t even cross&nbsp;
the 2% threshold to make the EDHRec page? Wild.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:40.320
This inclusion also brings us back to the&nbsp;
idea of footprint, how good it feels to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:45.040
devoting deck space to a given card. Mirror is&nbsp;
a great accompaniment to the Sibsig Ceremony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:50.640
but in a deck with 19 ETB creatures it’ll also&nbsp;
just feel solid in general most of the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:54.800
This fact is important because this deck is&nbsp;
designed in a way that the task of mitigating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.800 --> 00:13:00.080
The Sibsig Ceremony’s downside is nice, but not&nbsp;
essential. Obviously we won’t always have the card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:04.640
in play, and even if we do there are a single&nbsp;
digit number of creatures that we’ll be really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:10.160
bummed if we play into it, mainly engines&nbsp;
like Braids and Sun Titan. These do exist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.920
but because the number of them is small it&nbsp;
wouldn’t be worth the deck slot to run a card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.720
like Shielded by Faith that would only exist for&nbsp;
the purpose of mitigating Ceremony’s downside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:22.560
It also wouldn’t be worth it to try and&nbsp;
make that mitigating card feel more useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:26.320
by running additional creatures that we&nbsp;
don’t want The Sibsig Ceremony to bonk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:31.360
because then we’d be sad when we drew The&nbsp;
Sibsig Ceremony but not the mitigator. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:36.080
what we’re doing with Mirror of Life Trapping here&nbsp;
threads the needle perfectly—the deck is built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:41.360
to mostly feel good with the Sibsig Ceremony, and&nbsp;
to also feel decent with Mirror of Life Trapping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:45.840
and the fact that these pair with each other to&nbsp;
give the deck a bunch more flexibility is a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:50.720
extra sauce that pushes Mirror over the edge into&nbsp;
being a worthwhile inclusion. Another example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:55.040
this idea is Fanatical Devotion, the enchantment&nbsp;
I mentioned earlier that allows its controller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:59.920
to sacrifice a creature in order to regenerate a&nbsp;
creature. This is nice with The Sibsig Ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:04.160
since zombies generated by it can effectively pay&nbsp;
to prevent it from destroying future creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:08.880
but it’s also just a solid protection spell, and&nbsp;
it’s a sac outlet in a deck that can do some very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:13.440
juicy things with a sac outlet. None of these&nbsp;
three tasks would be good enough on their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:18.160
to justify giving them deck space, and even&nbsp;
doing two of the three might not be enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:22.320
but put them all together and you’ve got&nbsp;
a card that slots into the deck nicely.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:26.400
So at this point, we’ve constructed a deck that&nbsp;
feels decent without The Sibsig Ceremony in play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:31.120
and hopefully pretty great with it. However, I’ve&nbsp;
omitted arguably one of the most important parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:36.400
of a hidden commander deck—the tutors. There are&nbsp;
four of these, they slot nicely into the deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:41.760
since three of them are ETB creatures, and they&nbsp;
all have other useful targets to grab. And, that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:45.600
kinda all there is to say about them. It might&nbsp;
be a little odd to you that I saved them for last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:50.560
and spent all of 27 words telling you about them,&nbsp;
but in truth tutors shouldn’t be the focus of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:55.840
attention if you’re trying to decide if building&nbsp;
around a card in your 99 is feasible. You’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:59.840
probably want them if you want the card to be a&nbsp;
part of your plan more often, but focusing first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:04.160
on how the deck works without the card is going to&nbsp;
be your easiest road to having a deck that is fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:08.880
and flexible. I also skimmed over tutors because&nbsp;
this is the part of the video where I get to pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:12.640
the rug out from under this whole operation,&nbsp;
and tell you that both of the principles I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:18.320
mentioned in this video also apply to ordinary&nbsp;
commanders that live in the command zone as well.

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:23.040
Principle 1 was to run analogs, cards that do&nbsp;
an equivalent task as your hidden commander.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.320
The reason for that was that you won’t&nbsp;
always have access to your hidden commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:30.640
either due to removal or due to not finding&nbsp;
it, so it’s useful to be able to still have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:35.680
some access to whatever that card gives you.&nbsp;
That principle isn’t quite as important with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:39.520
your actual command zone since drawing&nbsp;
the card isn’t part of the equation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:44.640
but it still might be a genuinely useful piece&nbsp;
of advice. If your commander costs a lot of mana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:48.720
having cards that do something similar to what it&nbsp;
does at a lower cost might help you to have fewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:53.120
awkward periods in a game when you can’t quite&nbsp;
afford to cast the card. If your commander is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:56.800
creature, having a noncreature permanent that does&nbsp;
something similar could help if you’re playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.800 --> 00:16:02.320
against a deck that thrives on clearing out boards&nbsp;
of creatures. Having a deck that feels truly fluid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:06.960
and cohesive generally means having various&nbsp;
different options pointed in the same general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:12.800
gameplay direction, and this will often mean cards&nbsp;
sharing some of the functions of your commander.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:17.200
Principle 2 was to minimize the footprint&nbsp;
of synergizing cards, and this is the much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:22.000
more broadly applicable one in my mind. If you’re&nbsp;
adding cards to your deck that specifically play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:27.360
well with your commander, it’s good if those cards&nbsp;
are as flexible and multi-purpose as possible. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:33.200
I’m playing a Hakbal deck, Silvergill Mentor and&nbsp;
Jungleborn Pioneer will both pair fabulously with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:39.120
the commander, since they give me two bodies for&nbsp;
one card, and can be curved into Hakbal. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:44.080
these might not actually be the best inclusions,&nbsp;
because what if Hakbal gets removed and I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:47.920
feel like shelling out the command tax just yet?&nbsp;
What if I draw a different card I’d much rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:52.800
play on turn 4? What if I draw into some synergy&nbsp;
pieces that push advantage in a slightly different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:57.680
direction than my commander on my current board&nbsp;
state? What if I’m expecting a board wipe soon? If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:02.640
I add cards to my deck specifically to synergize&nbsp;
with my commander and that’s all they do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:08.320
I’m kind of forced into playing and replaying my&nbsp;
commander in order to make those cards useful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:12.320
even when it might be better to develop&nbsp;
other parts of my gameplan. This is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:17.200
the minimized footprint comes into play:&nbsp;
cards that do multiple things for your deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:22.320
feel useful in multiple situations—these give&nbsp;
you more freedom in how to play the game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:27.120
The easiest way to go with this is having cards&nbsp;
that both have a card type you want and also ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:31.040
draw, or remove something, but you can get&nbsp;
a lot more intricate with how you make cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:36.240
feel useful if you try. I do think some people&nbsp;
go too far and suggest that your deck should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:40.640
always be able to function just fine without&nbsp;
your commander, and this does feel a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:46.080
silly to say about the Commander format.&nbsp;
Still, making sure cards feel good across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:51.840
a variety of different situations, or are truly&nbsp;
powerful enough to compensate if they’re not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:56.000
is a good way to improve your decks&nbsp;
and your experience with the game.

